{"records":[{"id":"d8s22gp96w","title":"SZ XW","description":"Triple-negative breast cancer is a typical molecular subtype of breast cancer that lacks the expression of estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor and human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 which is also well known for its aggressive and metastatic clinical characteristics. Currently, no specific targeted therapy is available for TNBC. CD4+ T cells are critical regulators of immune responses but their role in breast cancer is currently unknown. Therefore, we investigated the gene expression profile of tumor infiltrating CD4+ T cells and to predict their potential roles in modulating antitumor immune function. As a result, abundant Treg and exhausted lymphocytes were detected, accompanied by largely decreased of effector/memory and cytotoxic T cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-21T08:45:02.977Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d8s22gp96w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d8s22gp96w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sha","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tumor Immune Microenvironment"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gg79sbwc4d","title":"Katzenellenbogen, B. Supplementary_Materials for ","description":"Western blots, RNA-seq analysis, and cell pathways and genes regulated by estrogen receptor beta 1","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-27T13:20:08.366Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gg79sbwc4d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gg79sbwc4d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Benita","last_name":"Katzenellenbogen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Estrogen Receptor","Western Blot","Gene Regulation","Cell Signaling Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nfftvjy5mh","title":"Supplemental Figure 3","description":"Supplemental Figure 3: Fitzpatrick skin type II, fragile nails after treatment with estrogen modulator examestane for breast cancer. Fingernails show onychoschizia at the distal edge.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-22T09:20:38.638Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nfftvjy5mh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nfftvjy5mh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"eden","last_name":"axler","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Nail Abnormality"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fznvffk3g8","title":"Data for: 17β-estradiol (E2) promotes growth and stability of new dendritic spines via estrogen receptor β pathway in intact mouse cortex","description":"Total statistical data demonstrates the dynamic dendritic plasticity in sensory cortex under different experimental conditions (estrogen/estrogen receptor agonist treatment) over various intervals.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-12-30T04:37:25.972Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fznvffk3g8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fznvffk3g8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"TONGHUI","last_name":"XU","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jun","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shaofang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dendritic Spines","Estrogen Receptor","Cerebral Cortex","Plasticity","Two-Photon Microscopy"],"journal_issn":["03619230"]},{"id":"t6m9hj4wm8","title":"Supplemental Figures for \"Relaxin Modulates the Genomic Actions and  Biological Effects of Estrogen in the Myometrium\" by Tripathy et al. (2024)","description":"This file contains Supplemental Figures 1-13 for \"Relaxin Modulates the Genomic Actions and  Biological Effects of Estrogen in the Myometrium\" by Tripathy et al. (2024) published in Endocrinology.\n\nEstradiol (E2) and relaxin (Rln) are steroid and polypeptide hormones, respectively, with important roles in the female reproductive tract, including myometrium.  Some actions of Rln, which are mediated by its membrane receptor RXFP1, require or are augmented by E2 signaling through its cognate nuclear steroid receptor, estrogen receptor alpha (ER).  In contrast, other actions of Rln act in opposition to the effects of E2.  Here we explored the molecular and genomic mechanisms that underlie the functional interplay between E2 and Rln in the myometrium.  We used both ovariectomized female mice and immortalized human myometrial cells expressing wild-type or mutant ER (hTERT-HM-ER cells).  Our results indicate that Rln modulates the genomic actions and biological effects of estrogen in the myometrium and myometrial cells by reducing phosphorylation of ER on serine 118 (S118), as well as by reducing the E2-dependent binding of ER across the genome.  These effects were associated with changes in the hormone-regulated transcriptome, including a decrease in the E2-dependent expression of some genes and enhanced expression of others.  The inhibitory effects of Rln cotreatment on the E2-dependent phosphorylation of ER required the nuclear dual-specificity phosphatases DUSP1 and DUSP5.  Moreover, the inhibitory effects of Rln were reflected in a concomitant inhibition of the E2-dependent contraction of myometrial cells.  Collectively, our results identify a pathway that integrates Rln/RXFP1 and E2/ER signaling, resulting in a convergence of membrane and nuclear signaling pathways to control genomic and biological outcomes.\n\nKEYWORDS\nEstrogen, estradiol, relaxin, dual-specificity phosphatase, myometrium, estrogen receptor, relaxin receptor, contraction.\n\nABBREVIATIONS\nE2, estradiol; Rln, relaxin; RXFP1, relaxin family peptide receptor 1 (relaxin receptor); DUSP, dual-specificity phosphatase; ChIP, chromatin immunoprecipitation; RT-qPCR, reverse transcription-quantitative PCR.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-04T19:31:35.337Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t6m9hj4wm8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t6m9hj4wm8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"W. Lee","last_name":"Kraus","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00adh9b73","name":"National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases","grant_id":"R01 DK058110"}],"categories":["Endocrinology","Genomics","Estrogen Receptor","Relaxin","Biomedical Research"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w57dst72t8","title":"Data for: Deep Ranking Analysis by Power Eigenvectors (DRAPE): a polypharmacology case study","description":"A dataset comprising 55 molecules described by seven criteria was used. The criteria are composed of binding activity values for each target expressed as half maximal activity concentration (AC50), based on the dose-response curves, thus the smaller the concentration, the more active the molecules. Seven targets are taken into account belonging to the nuclear receptors family: Estrogen Receptor Alpha (ERα), Estrogen Receptor Beta (ERβ), Farnesoid X Receptor (FXR), Progesterone Receptor (PR), Pregnane X Receptor (PXR), Peroxisome Proliferator-Activated Receptor Gamma (PPARγ) and Peroxisome Proliferator-Activated Receptor Delta (PPARδ). To create the dataset we collected  from [22] the Tox21 databases [23, 24] of agonism/antagonism activity for the seven nuclear receptors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-14T12:13:12.904Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w57dst72t8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w57dst72t8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Roberto","last_name":"Todeschini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Viviana","last_name":"Consonni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"davide","last_name":"ballabio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cecile","last_name":"Valsecchi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8fh6244bvx","title":"Expression pattern of estrogen-related receptors in the nervous system of adult and embryonic rats","description":"As an orphan nuclear receptor with unknown ligand. estrogen-related receptors (ERRs) are involved in the complex signal transduction system of estrogen, and ERRs may also play a regulatory role in the occurrence and development of hormone-dependent diseases such as breast cancer and osteoporosis. But so far, there is no report about the expression, localization and function of ERRs in the development and maturation process of nervous system. In the present study, we detected the expression and localization of ERRs in the nervous system of adult and embryonic rats. The results showed that the expression of ERRs exhibited dynamic changes in both time and space in growth and development process of brain and spinal cord. This study systematically showed the expression status of ERRs in the nervous system of adult and embryonic rats, which provided an important morphological basis for the in-depth study of the function of ERRs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-23T14:22:30.052Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8fh6244bvx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8fh6244bvx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shanyong","last_name":"Yi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bdp25zzbrn","title":"Data for: Estrogenic Phytochemical from Labisia pumila (Myrsinaceae) with Selectivity towards Estrogen Receptor Alpha and Beta Subtypes","description":"NMR spectra of alkyl resorcinols and a dimeric 1,4-benzoquinone from Labisia pumila. Inclusive of graphs of concentration-response curve for estrogen receptor alpha and beta binding assay and Cytotoxic HL60 assay.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-07-18T16:13:20.408Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bdp25zzbrn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bdp25zzbrn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chee Yan","last_name":"Choo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Musthahimah","last_name":"Muhamad","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tomoyo","last_name":"Hasuda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"YUKIO","last_name":"HITOTSUYANAGI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Phytochemistry"],"journal_issn":["0367326X"]},{"id":"v25sw2s8k9","title":"Combined inhibition of KAT6A/B and Menin reverses estrogen receptor-driven gene expression programs in breast cancer","description":"Immunoblot and microscopy images for Olsen et al. 2025 Cell Reports Medicine","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-21T08:01:14.881Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v25sw2s8k9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v25sw2s8k9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sarah Naomi","last_name":"Olsen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Armstrong","orcid":"0000-0002-9099-4728"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Education by Subject"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xkk778tsj4","title":"ESWT reduces cartilage degeneration","description":"Data related to the article entitled \"Extracorporeal shockwave therapy reduces the deleterious effects of estrogen deprivation in the articular cartilage of rats\".\n- Histomorphometry \n- Immunohistochemistry \n- Molecular Biology\n- Densitometry Measurements (DXA)\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-24T12:22:45.858Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xkk778tsj4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xkk778tsj4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aline Gomes Hidalgo","last_name":"Mierzwa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Isabela ","last_name":"Capucho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jenifer Freitas","last_name":"Campos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paulo Roberto Dias dos","last_name":"Santos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Flávio","last_name":"Faloppa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Helena Bonciani","last_name":"Nader","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rejane Daniele ","last_name":"Reginato","orcid":"0000-0002-4370-9111"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Histology","Cell Death","Dual-Energy X-Ray Absorptiometry","Shock Wave","Ovariectomized Animal","Cartilage Matrix","Articular Cartilage","Animal Osteoarthritis","Cartilage Repair"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":134679,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":125,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":134679,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":134135,"restricted":67,"embargoed":477},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2304},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1344},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":646},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":470},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":435},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":374},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":306},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":273}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":132040},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1906},{"id":"a191461c-022